window.__hvBdRegister("plut-lives-2", {"key":"plut-lives-2","sections":[{"i":0,"j":29,"loc":"Life of Alcibiades, chs. 1–9","title":"Alcibiades: family, beauty, Socrates, and youthful insolence","summary":"Plutarch traces Alcibiades' descent from Eurysaces and the Alcmaeonids and his upbringing as the ward of Pericles. He describes his beauty, his charming lisp, and a boyhood driven by love of rivalry: biting like a lion in wrestling, lying down before a waggon, and rejecting the flute as ignoble. Socrates' love for him is shown as a rival to his flatterers; Alcibiades reveres Socrates while insulting other lovers such as Anytus, and the two save each other at Potidaea and Delium. The section closes with his wanton acts: striking Hipponicus and then marrying Hipparete, dragging her back from the divorce court, and docking the tail of his costly dog so Athens would talk of that.","ideas":["Love of rivalry and preeminence as his ruling passions","Socrates' love as a corrective to flattery and pride","Potidaea and Delium: mutual rescue of teacher and pupil","Calculated outrages to manage his reputation"],"terms":[{"t":"philotimia (love of honour)","d":"The ambition and love of distinction that Plutarch treats as the strongest of Alcibiades' passions."},{"t":"erastēs (lover)","d":"The older admirer of a youth; Socrates is shown as a lover seeking the soul's good, unlike the others."}]},{"i":30,"j":64,"loc":"Life of Alcibiades, chs. 10–16","title":"Alcibiades enters public life and dazzles Athens","summary":"Alcibiades enters politics by a public contribution, made memorable by an escaped quail, and relies on eloquence, though he sometimes halts in search of the right word. His seven chariots at Olympia and the cities' rivalry to serve him bring glory, along with the charge that he cheated Diomedes of a team. He joins Nicias to turn the ostracism onto Hyperbolus, then, jealous of the Peace of Nicias, tricks the Spartan envoys into denying their full powers and wins the Argive alliance that leads to Mantinea. Plutarch sets his statecraft against his luxury and lawlessness: the golden shield with Eros, the imprisoned painter, his support for the Melian decree, and Timon's remark that he will grow big enough to ruin the people.","ideas":["Olympic victory with seven chariots","Ostracism turned against Hyperbolus","Deception of the Spartan embassy and the Argive alliance","Athenians both yearn for and fear him"],"terms":[{"t":"ostrakismos (ostracism)","d":"The Athenian vote banishing an over-powerful citizen, which here falls for the first time on a worthless man, Hyperbolus."},{"t":"Peace of Nicias","d":"The treaty between Athens and Sparta credited to Nicias, which Alcibiades works to break."}]},{"i":65,"j":84,"loc":"Life of Alcibiades, chs. 17–22","title":"The Sicilian expedition, the Hermae, and recall","summary":"Alcibiades fires Athens with hopes of conquering Sicily, and beyond it Carthage and Libya, while Socrates and Meton foresee no good. Nicias and Lamachus are made his colleagues, and ill omens follow: the Adonia and the mutilation of the Hermae. Enemies accuse him of parodying the Eleusinian mysteries, but contrive to have him sail with the charge pending; in his absence informers multiply, Andocides gives evidence, and the Salaminian galley is sent to bring him home. Alcibiades betrays a plot at Messana, escapes at Thurii, and is condemned to death in absence, his property confiscated and his name cursed, though the priestess Theano refuses.","ideas":["Grand ambitions for Sicily and Carthage","Mutilation of the Hermae and the mysteries charge","Trial deferred by his enemies' design","Flight and condemnation: 'I'll show them that I'm alive'"],"terms":[{"t":"Hermae","d":"Pillar-statues of Hermes at Athenian doorways, whose mass mutilation was taken as a sign of conspiracy."},{"t":"Eleusinian mysteries","d":"Secret rites of Demeter and Cora that Alcibiades was accused of mimicking at home."}]},{"i":85,"j":99,"loc":"Life of Alcibiades, chs. 23–27","title":"Exile at Sparta, Tissaphernes, and return to command","summary":"At Sparta Alcibiades urges sending Gylippus to Syracuse and fortifying Deceleia, adopting Spartan austerity with a chameleon's adaptability, while seducing King Agis' wife Timaea. Facing Spartan orders for his death, he flees to the satrap Tissaphernes, advising him to let the Greeks wear each other down. He courts the oligarchs at Samos, outwits Phrynichus, and helps bring about the Four Hundred, who then ignore him. The army at Samos elects him general; he restrains them from sailing against Athens, and on his return to the war he decides the battle off Abydos, only to be imprisoned by Tissaphernes at Sardis.","ideas":["Deceleia and Gylippus: harm done to Athens from Sparta","The chameleon: imitating every way of life","Balancing Persia between the warring Greeks","Preventing civil war from Samos"],"terms":[{"t":"Deceleia","d":"A fort in Attica occupied by Sparta on Alcibiades' advice, from which the enemy controlled the countryside."},{"t":"the Four Hundred","d":"The oligarchy set up at Athens on the pretext of winning Persian support through Alcibiades."}]},{"i":100,"j":119,"loc":"Life of Alcibiades, chs. 28–34","title":"Cyzicus, the Hellespont campaigns, and triumphant homecoming","summary":"Escaping Sardis, Alcibiades uses a rainstorm to surprise Mindarus at Cyzicus, destroying the Peloponnesian fleet and killing its admiral. He reconciles his men with Thrasyllus' troops, defeats Pharnabazus, takes Chalcedon's goods, bluffs his way into Selymbria, and captures Byzantium by a feigned departure. He then sails home with captured figure-heads and is welcomed by crowds; Plutarch doubts Duris' tale of flutes and purple sails. Elected sole general and freed from the curses, he leads the Eleusinian procession overland past the enemy, and some of the poor wish to make him tyrant.","ideas":["Victory at Cyzicus and the laconic dispatch","Stratagems at Selymbria and Byzantium","Return on the ill-omened day of the Plynteria","Restoring the land procession to Eleusis"],"terms":[{"t":"Plynteria","d":"An Athenian festival when Athena's image was veiled, held to be the unluckiest day for any business."},{"t":"Iacchus procession","d":"The festal march from Athens to Eleusis, suspended during the Deceleian occupation and restored by Alcibiades."}]},{"i":120,"j":132,"loc":"Life of Alcibiades, chs. 35–39","title":"Second disgrace, Aegospotami, and death in Phrygia","summary":"Influential Athenians, fearing tyranny, send Alcibiades away; his failure at Andros and his absences to raise money feed suspicion, since his reputation made every failure look deliberate. His lieutenant Antiochus disobeys orders and is defeated by Lysander, and Thrasybulus' denunciation leads Athens to depose him. He warns the generals at Aegospotami of their poor anchorage but is rebuffed, and Lysander destroys the fleet and takes Athens. Withdrawing to Pharnabazus, he is killed on Spartan prompting: after a dream of wearing Timandra's garments, he dashes from his burning house and falls to arrows, and Timandra buries him.","ideas":["Ruined by his own reputation for success","Ignored warning before Aegospotami","The Thirty and Lysander fear him even in exile","Death by fire and arrows; alternative account of a wronged family"],"terms":[{"t":"Aegospotami","d":"The Hellespont anchorage where Lysander captured almost the whole Athenian fleet."}]},{"i":133,"j":153,"loc":"Life of Coriolanus, chs. 1–11","title":"Caius Marcius: upbringing, valour, and the name Coriolanus","summary":"Caius Marcius, of the patrician Marcii, is raised by his widowed mother; Plutarch says his noble but undisciplined nature produced great deeds alongside a harsh, arrogant temper. He wins the oak crown in his first battle against Tarquin and seeks glory chiefly to gladden his mother Volumnia. During the plebeian secession Menenius Agrippa tells the fable of the belly and the members, and tribunes are created, though Marcius opposes concessions. At Corioli he bursts through the gate, then hurries to the consul's battle against the Antiates; he refuses a tenth of the spoils, asks only freedom for a Volscian guest-friend, and receives the surname Coriolanus, which leads Plutarch into a digression on Roman names.","ideas":["Nature without culture: great virtue mixed with harshness","Valour pursued for his mother's joy","Fable of the belly; creation of the tribunate","Capture of Corioli and refusal of rewards"],"terms":[{"t":"virtus (manly valour)","d":"The Latin word for virtue, which Plutarch notes properly means military courage."},{"t":"corona civica (civic crown)","d":"The oak-leaf garland given for saving a citizen's life in battle."},{"t":"tribuni plebis (tribunes of the people)","d":"Officers elected to protect the plebeians, granted to end the secession."}]},{"i":154,"j":169,"loc":"Life of Coriolanus, chs. 12–20","title":"Famine, failed consulship, and condemnation to exile","summary":"After the war the popular leaders revive strife amid famine; Marcius forces through a colony to plague-stricken Velitrae and leads a private raid on Antium. Standing for consul, he first wins the people with his scars but loses when he arrives escorted by the senate, and Plutarch faults his lack of a statesman's gentleness. When grain arrives from Syracuse he urges the senate to refuse cheap grain and abolish the tribunate, and the tribunes seek to hurl him from the Tarpeian rock. A trial follows: voting by tribes, the tribunes drop the charge of tyranny for his speech and his distribution of Antine spoils, and he is banished for life by three tribes.","ideas":["Bribery's slow corruption of Roman elections","Self-will as 'companion of solitude'","Speech against the grain distribution and tribunate","Trial by tribes and perpetual banishment"],"terms":[{"t":"Tarpeian rock","d":"The cliff from which condemned criminals were thrown, to which the tribunes first sentence Marcius."},{"t":"comitia by tribes","d":"Voting by tribes rather than centuries, which the tribunes insist on to give the poor the majority."}]},{"i":170,"j":187,"loc":"Life of Coriolanus, chs. 21–31","title":"Exile to Tullus and war against Rome","summary":"Calm in appearance but consumed by rage, Marcius leaves Rome and, disguised, sits as a suppliant at the hearth of his old enemy Tullus Aufidius, offering his service against Rome. At Rome a dream of Titus Latinus about a bad dancer leads to renewed games, and Plutarch digresses on Roman ritual scruple. Marcius provokes war by a false alarm, raids Roman land while sparing patrician estates, and takes Circeii, Bola, and other Latin towns. The senate vetoes his recall; he encamps five miles from Rome, sets terms for the Volscians, grants thirty days, and rejects a second embassy, while Tullus grows jealous.","ideas":["Anger as transmuted pain","Suppliant at an enemy's hearth","Sparing patrician lands to divide Rome","Encampment at the Fossae Cluiliae and harsh terms"],"terms":[{"t":"Hoc age","d":"The herald's cry 'Mind this!' ordered by Numa to keep attention fixed on sacred rites."},{"t":"furcifer","d":"A slave punished by carrying the waggon prop (furca), a mark of lasting disgrace."}]},{"i":188,"j":217,"loc":"Life of Coriolanus, chs. 32–39","title":"Volumnia's plea and the death of Coriolanus","summary":"After even the priests fail, Plutarch digresses on Homer: gods do not remove human choice but prompt it through motives and conceptions. Valeria, so moved, leads the women to Volumnia, who goes with Vergilia and the children to the Volscian camp. Volumnia argues that he cannot attack Rome without treading on her corpse and finally kneels; Marcius yields, saying her victory is his ruin, and withdraws. Rome builds a temple of Women's Fortune, whose statue is said to speak, which Plutarch doubts. Back at Antium, Tullus' faction kills Marcius before he can make his defence; the Volscians honour his tomb, and soon fall subject to Rome.","ideas":["Divine influence works through motives, not compulsion","Volumnia's speech and kneeling plea","Talking statues judged impossible as literal speech","Murder at Antium and Volscian decline"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna Muliebris (Women's Fortune)","d":"The goddess whose temple the women asked for as their reward for saving Rome."}]},{"i":218,"j":226,"loc":"Comparison of Alcibiades and Coriolanus","title":"Comparing Alcibiades and Coriolanus","summary":"Plutarch judges the two nearly equal as soldiers, though Alcibiades won more on land and sea. As statesmen, Alcibiades' wantonness offended the sober and Marcius' arrogance offended the people; courting the many is held less blameworthy than insulting them. Both used deceit to make war, but Marcius acted from anger, harmed his whole city, and abandoned the Volscians who trusted him, while Alcibiades returned to help Athens. Marcius was incorruptible about money but lacked persuasion, so he was admired yet unloved; his pride made him resent the honours he claimed to despise.","ideas":["Flattering the people versus coercing them","Anger as the worse motive for treachery","Sparing Rome for his mother alone dishonoured his country","Marcius' temperance against Alcibiades' greed"],"terms":[{"t":"authadeia (self-will)","d":"The obstinate, solitary pride Plutarch, citing Plato, sees as Marcius' chief fault."}]},{"i":227,"j":239,"loc":"Life of Timoleon, pr.–ch. 7","title":"Sicily's plight and Timoleon's fratricide","summary":"Syracuse, after Dion's murder, passes from tyrant to tyrant; Dionysius returns, the nobles turn to Hicetas, who secretly deals with Carthage while joining the appeal to Corinth. Corinth unexpectedly chooses Timoleon, a gentle hater of tyrants who once saved his brother Timophanes in battle. When Timophanes makes himself tyrant, Timoleon stands weeping while his companions kill him; his mother's curses drive him into twenty years of withdrawal, and Telecleides warns he will be called tyrannicide or fratricide by the result.","ideas":["Hicetas' double dealing with Carthage","Timoleon sets country above family","Choices must rest on firm reason, not praise or blame"],"terms":[{"t":"metameleia (repentance)","d":"Regret after acting, which Plutarch says can make even a noble deed base if the conviction behind it was weak."}]},{"i":240,"j":252,"loc":"Life of Timoleon, chs. 7–15","title":"Omens, the crossing to Sicily, and Dionysius' surrender","summary":"Hicetas openly sides with Carthage and tells Corinth to stay home, which only spurs the expedition. Omens attend it: the goddesses of Persephone in dreams, a victory fillet falling on Timoleon at Delphi, and a torch in the sky. At Rhegium Timoleon tricks the Carthaginians with a long assembly and slips across to Tauromenium, where Andromachus receives him. With 1,200 men he routs Hicetas' 5,000 at Adranum, and Dionysius surrenders the citadel and is sent to Corinth, where Plutarch records his humbled life and sayings, including Diogenes' rebuke.","ideas":["Divine signs for the voyage","Escape from Rhegium by stratagem","Victory at Adranum against odds","Dionysius in Corinth: a lesson of fortune"],"terms":[{"t":"tychē (fortune)","d":"The force Plutarch repeatedly credits alongside Timoleon's valour for his successes."}]},{"i":253,"j":265,"loc":"Life of Timoleon, chs. 16–23","title":"Taking Syracuse and refounding the city","summary":"Within fifty days the citadel is Timoleon's; an assassination attempt at Adranum fails when one assassin is killed by a man avenging his father, an example of fortune's weaving. Hicetas brings in Mago with 150 ships and 60,000 troops, but Neon seizes Achradina, Corinthian reinforcements cross the calm strait, and talk among the mercenaries fishing for eels makes Mago suspect treachery and sail away. Timoleon storms the city without losing a man, demolishes the tyrants' citadel and tombs, and builds courts there. With Syracuse empty, Corinth invites settlers and exiles; the land is divided, houses sold, and only Gelon's statue spared.","ideas":["Fortune's design in the foiled assassination","Mago's flight from Syracuse","Demolition of the tyrants' stronghold","Corinth's proclamation inviting colonists"],"terms":[{"t":"Achradina","d":"The strongest quarter of Syracuse, seized by Neon and joined to the citadel."}]},{"i":266,"j":272,"loc":"Life of Timoleon, chs. 24–29","title":"The battle of the Crimesus","summary":"Timoleon forces Hicetas to submit, sends Leptines to Corinth, and sets his mercenaries raiding Carthaginian territory. Carthage lands 70,000 men at Lilybaeum, and a thousand of his mercenaries desert. Timoleon turns an omen of parsley into the Isthmian victor's crown and attacks as the enemy cross the Crimesus, sending cavalry against the chariots. A storm of thunder, hail, and rain strikes the Carthaginians in the face, and their heavy armour bogs them in the mud; ten thousand fall, three thousand native Carthaginians, and Timoleon sends the finest spoils to Corinth.","ideas":["Parsley reread as a victory wreath","Attack while the enemy is split by the river","Storm decides the battle","Barbarian spoils dedicated at Corinth"],"terms":[{"t":"selinon (parsley)","d":"A plant used to wreath tombs and also the Isthmian victors' crown, hence the double-edged omen."}]},{"i":273,"j":292,"loc":"Life of Timoleon, chs. 30–39","title":"Last tyrants, settled Sicily, and Timoleon's death","summary":"The deserters are killed by the Bruttians, and the temple-robbing mercenaries perish piecemeal, which Plutarch reads as justice sparing Timoleon. He defeats Hicetas at the Damurias, executes him, and allows the Syracusans to kill his women, the one blot Plutarch records; Mamercus and Hippo are taken and killed, and Carthage makes peace. Sicily is repopulated and Timoleon, attributing all to Chance, stays in Syracuse, welcoming free speech even from accusers. Going blind, he lays down command but is brought to the assembly for great matters; at his death Syracuse buries him publicly and founds the Timoleonteum.","ideas":["Divine justice on the sacrilegious mercenaries","Execution of Hicetas' family as a stain","Effortless success compared to Homer's poetry","Blind, honoured counsellor; public burial"],"terms":[{"t":"Automatia (Chance)","d":"The power to which Timoleon built a shrine in his house, crediting fortune with his deeds."},{"t":"Timoleonteum","d":"The gymnasium built around his tomb in the Syracusan market place."}]},{"i":293,"j":305,"loc":"Life of Aemilius Paulus, chs. 1–6","title":"Preface, family, and early career of Aemilius","summary":"Plutarch explains that he writes the Lives as a mirror for shaping his own character, and pairs Timoleon with Aemilius Paulus as men alike in virtue and good fortune. He traces the Aemilii, the death of Aemilius's father at Cannae, and Aemilius's choice to seek fame through valour, justice, and trustworthiness rather than through court pleading and canvassing. As aedile and augur he is strict in religious and military custom; as praetor he wins in Spain without enriching himself. His divorce of Papiria prompts the parable of the shoe that pinches, and his family ties, including the poor but virtuous Aelius Tubero, are described. As consul he defeats the Ligurians, then, passed over for a second consulship, devotes himself to his augurate and to educating his sons in Roman and Greek learning.","ideas":["History as a mirror for moral self-improvement","Strictness in small rites guards against greater lawlessness","Small, unseen frictions can end a marriage","Integrity in office: no profit from campaigns"],"terms":[{"t":"augur","d":"A Roman priest who interpreted divine will from the flight of birds and signs in the sky."},{"t":"aedileship","d":"The Roman magistracy Aemilius first sought, winning over twelve rivals."}]},{"i":306,"j":321,"loc":"Life of Aemilius Paulus, chs. 7–16","title":"Perseus of Macedon and Aemilius takes command","summary":"Rome's generals have fared badly against Perseus, so Plutarch sketches the Macedonian dynasty from Antigonus to Philip, who secretly rebuilt Macedonian strength after defeat by Flamininus. Perseus, said to be of doubtful birth and chiefly marked by avarice, wages war with some success. The Romans recall the sixty-year-old Aemilius to a second consulship; his daughter's report that 'our Perseus' (a pet dog) is dead is taken as an omen, and he tells the people not to try to command their commander. Perseus loses the Bastarnae and cheats Genthius out of promised money. Aemilius disciplines his army, finds water by digging at the foot of Olympus (with a digression on how springs arise), and sends Nasica on a night march that turns Perseus's position, forcing him back toward Pydna.","ideas":["Perseus's avarice undoes his own war effort","Aemilius demands obedience, not advice, from the people and soldiers","Digression on whether spring water is stored or generated underground","Nasica's flanking march over the Olympus pass"],"terms":[{"t":"Basternae","d":"Danubian Gallic horsemen whom Perseus hired and then dismissed rather than pay them."}]},{"i":322,"j":337,"loc":"Life of Aemilius Paulus, chs. 17–26","title":"The battle of Pydna and Perseus's surrender","summary":"Aemilius declines to fight straight off the march and camps; that night a lunar eclipse alarms the Macedonians, while he sacrifices and waits for favourable omens. The battle begins by accident in the afternoon; the Macedonian phalanx at first seems unstoppable and drives back the Pelignians, but on uneven ground it opens gaps, and Aemilius sends his cohorts into them and breaks it. Plutarch weighs Polybius's charge that Perseus fled from cowardice against Poseidonius's defence, recounts young Cato's recovery of his sword, and describes the night search for the missing young Scipio. Perseus flees to Pella and Samothrace, cheated by Cretans; reports of the victory reach Rome miraculously fast. Captured, he grovels before Aemilius, who rebukes his cowardice.","ideas":["Lunar eclipse read as an omen for the king","Phalanx broken by attacks into its gaps","Rumours of victory that outrun messengers","Valour in misfortune earns respect; cowardice forfeits pity"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"The Macedonian close formation of long spears, invincible on level ground but vulnerable when broken up."},{"t":"Dioscuri","d":"Castor and Pollux, at whose Samothracian temple Perseus took refuge and who once brought news of a victory to Rome."}]},{"i":338,"j":344,"loc":"Life of Aemilius Paulus, chs. 27–31","title":"Fortune's lesson, settlement, and the triumph dispute","summary":"Aemilius warns the young officers in his tent that Perseus's fall shows how unstable Fortune is and bids them curb their pride. He tours Greece, restores governments, hosts games with careful courtesy, refuses to touch the royal treasure, and lets his sons take only the king's books. On the senate's order he sacks seventy cities of Epirus in a single hour, a deed contrary to his nature that yields each soldier little. Back in Rome, his soldiers, resenting the meagre booty, are stirred by Servius Galba to vote against his triumph. Marcus Servilius halts the vote with a speech displaying his wounds and shames the army.","ideas":["Victory as a reminder of human weakness","Ordering a battle and a banquet need the same spirit","The sack of Epirus as a harsh commission","Soldiers' greed nearly denies the triumph"],"terms":[{"t":"tychē (Fortune)","d":"The changeable power Aemilius sees as governing the rise and fall of kings and conquerors."}]},{"i":345,"j":353,"loc":"Life of Aemilius Paulus, chs. 32–39","title":"The triumph, loss of sons, and death","summary":"The three-day triumph displays statues, Macedonian arms, coined silver and gold, Perseus's chariot, and his weeping children, followed by Perseus himself and then Aemilius in his chariot. Plutarch notes a divine power that diminishes excessive prosperity: Aemilius's two younger sons die, one five days before and the other three days after the triumph. Aemilius tells the people that he had feared Fortune's reversal and now judges that his private grief has paid for the city's success. Perseus dies in captivity; Aemilius's spoils relieve Romans of special taxes, he holds a moderate censorship while siding with the aristocracy yet loved by the people, and he dies after an illness, his bier carried by former enemies.","ideas":["The triumph as spectacle mingled with pity","Private sorrow offsetting public success","Popular yet aristocratic in politics","Honoured at death by Iberians, Ligurians, and Macedonians"],"terms":[{"t":"triumph","d":"The Roman victory procession, here lasting three days."},{"t":"censorship","d":"The office reviewing citizens' conduct and the rolls; Aemilius exercised it with moderation."}]},{"i":354,"j":358,"loc":"Comparison of Timoleon and Aemilius","title":"Comparison of Timoleon and Aemilius Paulus","summary":"Plutarch finds few differences between the two: both fought notable enemies and won famous victories. Timoleon may deserve more credit for winning with mercenaries against a large Carthaginian force, while Aemilius faced a stronger Perseus with disciplined troops. Both were incorruptible, but Aemilius's honesty came from Roman law and custom, Timoleon's from himself, amid Greek leaders in Sicily who were nearly all corrupt. Aemilius is praised for taking nothing from the conquered treasure, and judged the more perfect character for bearing his sons' deaths with dignity, whereas Timoleon was crushed for twenty years by grief over his brother.","ideas":["Equal success with unequal means favours the commander","Virtue from custom versus virtue from oneself","Refusing what one may lawfully take is the higher virtue","Steadiness in both prosperity and adversity"],"terms":[]},{"i":359,"j":370,"loc":"Life of Pelopidas, chs. 1–6","title":"Preface on daring; Pelopidas and Epaminondas","summary":"Plutarch opens with the difference between valuing courage and valuing life cheaply, arguing that a general, on whom all depend, should not risk himself needlessly; Pelopidas and Marcellus are paired as great men who fell rashly in battle. Pelopidas, rich and generous, shares the simple life of his poor friend Epaminondas, and the two remain without rivalry through their careers, a friendship sealed when Epaminondas defended the wounded Pelopidas at Mantineia. The Spartans, at the urging of Theban oligarchs, seize the Cadmeia; Ismenias is executed and Pelopidas and others are exiled to Athens. The Athenians refuse Spartan demands to expel them.","ideas":["A general's first duty is to preserve himself for others","Mastery of wealth rather than slavery to it","A friendship free of envy between colleagues","The Spartan seizure of the Cadmeia"],"terms":[{"t":"Cadmeia","d":"The citadel of Thebes, seized and garrisoned by the Spartans."},{"t":"boeotarch","d":"One of the chief elected magistrates and generals of the Boeotian league."}]},{"i":371,"j":382,"loc":"Life of Pelopidas, chs. 7–13","title":"The liberation of Thebes from the Spartans","summary":"Pelopidas urges the exiles to imitate Thrasybulus and free Thebes; Charon offers his house and Phillidas, secretary to the polemarchs, arranges a drinking party. Twelve young exiles slip into the city disguised as hunters and peasants; a messenger sent to call off the plot is stopped by a quarrel over a bridle. Charon, summoned by Archias, leaves his son as a pledge and bluffs his way through, and Archias puts aside a letter revealing the plot with the words 'serious business for the morrow'. Disguised as women, the conspirators kill Archias and Philip, while Pelopidas kills Leontidas in a hard fight. The citizens are armed, the assembly hails them as deliverers, and the Spartan garrison surrenders.","ideas":["A handful of men in a private house break Spartan supremacy","Chance nearly ruins and then saves the enterprise","Charon's pledge of his own son","The exploit compared to that of Thrasybulus"],"terms":[{"t":"polemarchs","d":"The pro-Spartan Theban magistrates Archias and Philip, killed at the banquet."},{"t":"harmost","d":"A Spartan governor of a subject city; three were punished after Thebes was freed."}]},{"i":383,"j":397,"loc":"Life of Pelopidas, chs. 14–22","title":"Tegyra, the Sacred Band, and the Leuctra dream","summary":"Pelopidas and Gorgidas provoke Sphodrias into a failed raid on the Piraeus, drawing Athens back into alliance with Thebes, and repeated small battles train the Thebans. At Tegyra, with the Sacred Band, Pelopidas routes a larger Spartan force, showing other Greeks that Spartans could be beaten; Plutarch digresses on Apollo's birthplace there. He describes the Sacred Band of three hundred, said to be made up of lovers, praised by Philip after Chaeroneia, and kept together by Pelopidas as a unit. Before Leuctra Pelopidas dreams that Scedasus demands a virgin sacrifice; the commanders debate human sacrifice, and a fiery-maned filly is offered instead.","ideas":["Tegyra as prelude to Leuctra","Love between comrades as a bond in battle","Debate over whether gods could want human sacrifice","A substitute victim sent by Heaven"],"terms":[{"t":"hieros lochos (Sacred Band)","d":"The Theban elite corps of three hundred chosen men, said to be pairs of lovers."},{"t":"Leuctridae","d":"The daughters of Scedasus, wronged by Spartans and buried at Leuctra."}]},{"i":398,"j":412,"loc":"Life of Pelopidas, chs. 23–29","title":"Leuctra, Peloponnese, trial, and Thessalian captivity","summary":"At Leuctra Pelopidas's rapid charge with the Sacred Band catches the Spartans changing formation while Epaminondas attacks obliquely, and the Spartans suffer an unprecedented rout. The two then invade Laconia and restore Messenia, keeping command beyond their legal term, for which both are tried and acquitted; Pelopidas later defeats Menecleidas's attempt to honour Charon alone. Pelopidas settles Thessaly and Macedonia, bringing the boy Philip to Thebes as a hostage. On a later embassy he is seized by Alexander of Pherae, defies the tyrant, and wins over the tyrant's wife Thebe. Epaminondas, cautious for his friend's safety, frightens Alexander into releasing him.","ideas":["The Sacred Band's charge decides Leuctra","Nature's law: men obey whoever can save them","Envy at home toward successful generals","Pelopidas's defiance of the tyrant in prison"],"terms":[{"t":"Alexander of Pherae","d":"The cruel Thessalian tyrant who imprisoned Pelopidas."}]},{"i":413,"j":421,"loc":"Life of Pelopidas, chs. 30–35","title":"Embassy to Persia, death at Cynoscephalae","summary":"Pelopidas goes as ambassador to Artaxerxes, who honours him and grants Theban demands, while Pelopidas accepts only token gifts, unlike the Athenian Timagoras. Called back to Thessaly against Alexander, he sets out despite a solar eclipse with three hundred volunteer horsemen. At Cynoscephalae he wins the heights, but on seeing Alexander he charges ahead in anger and is killed by the mercenaries, though his cavalry routs the enemy. The Thessalians mourn him extravagantly and beg to bury him; Plutarch praises the voluntary honour of the funeral over the forced pomp of tyrants. The Thebans compel Alexander's submission, and Thebe and her brothers later kill him in his bed.","ideas":["Honour from a king without bribery","Anger overrides judgement and costs Pelopidas his life","Unforced grief as the truest funeral honour","The tyrant slain by his own wife's plot"],"terms":[]},{"i":422,"j":435,"loc":"Life of Marcellus, chs. 1–8","title":"Marcellus's youth and the spolia opima","summary":"Marcus Claudius Marcellus, five times consul, is warlike yet humane and an admirer of Greek learning; he excels in single combat and saves his brother in Sicily. As aedile he prosecutes his colleague Capitolinus for advances to his son. Rome renews war with the Gauls amid fear and omens; the consul Flaminius is forced from office for ignoring recall letters, and Plutarch gives examples of Roman strictness about rites. As consul Marcellus rides against the Gaesatae at Clastidium, turns a shying horse into an act of worship, kills king Britomartus in single combat, and dedicates his armour to Jupiter Feretrius, the third and last Roman to do so.","ideas":["A warlike nature joined with humanity","Roman scrupulousness about omens and ritual","Victory at Clastidium by few horsemen","The third and last dedication of spolia opima"],"terms":[{"t":"spolia opima","d":"Spoils taken when a Roman general kills an enemy commander in battle, dedicated to Jupiter Feretrius."},{"t":"Gaesatae","d":"Mercenary Gauls from beyond the Alps who joined the Insubrians."}]},{"i":436,"j":448,"loc":"Life of Marcellus, chs. 9–17","title":"Against Hannibal at Nola; Archimedes at Syracuse","summary":"After Cannae Marcellus becomes Rome's 'sword' beside Fabius's 'shield'; he wins over Bantius of Nola with flattery and gifts, and, sallying from three gates, gives Hannibal's men their first defeat. He refuses a consulship marred by thunder and wins another battle, prompting deserters from Hannibal. Sent to Sicily, he pleads for the disgraced survivors of Cannae, takes Leontini, and besieges Syracuse. Plutarch explains how Archimedes, at Hiero's urging, applied geometry to machines, recalls Plato's objection to mechanics, and describes the engines that wreck the Roman fleet and drive off their sambuca. Marcellus jokes about the 'geometrical Briareus' and turns to blockade; Archimedes is said to have prized pure theory above all.","ideas":["Fabius the shield, Marcellus the sword","Hannibal's army shown to be beatable","Plato's objection to mechanics corrupting geometry","Archimedes's machines as the soul of the defence"],"terms":[{"t":"sambuca","d":"Marcellus's siege engine mounted on linked ships, named for its resemblance to a harp."},{"t":"mean proportionals","d":"The geometrical problem Eudoxus and Archytas solved with mechanical devices."}]},{"i":449,"j":462,"loc":"Life of Marcellus, chs. 18–23","title":"Fall of Syracuse and its aftermath","summary":"Marcellus takes other Sicilian cities and, noticing a poorly guarded tower during talks over a prisoner, scales the walls during a festival of Artemis. He weeps over Syracuse's coming fate and forbids harm to free citizens while permitting plunder. Archimedes, absorbed in a diagram, is killed by a soldier, to Marcellus's grief. Plutarch tells how Nicias of Engyium escaped by feigning possession by the Mothers and later won mercy for his city. Marcellus brings Greek art to Rome, praised by the people but blamed by elders who preferred Fabius's restraint; he accepts a lesser triumph, prompting a digression on the ovation, and calmly faces Syracusan accusers in the senate, is acquitted, and is reconciled with them.","ideas":["Mercy within the sack of a city","The death of Archimedes in three versions","Greek art in Rome as refinement or corruption","Major triumph versus the ovation"],"terms":[{"t":"ovatio (ovation)","d":"The lesser triumph on foot with flutes and myrtle, said to be named from the sheep sacrificed."}]},{"i":463,"j":476,"loc":"Life of Marcellus, chs. 24–30","title":"Last campaigns against Hannibal and death","summary":"Rejecting Fabius's delaying strategy, Marcellus seeks battle, fights Hannibal to a draw at Numistro, and is ordered to name Quintus Fulvius dictator, prompting a note on the word. At Canusium he is defeated, rebukes his army, and next day routs Hannibal with the elephants turned back on their own lines. Accused by Bibulus of idleness, he is acquitted and made consul a fifth time, though portents delay him. Eager for a decisive battle, he rides out to inspect a hill despite bad omens, is ambushed, and killed with his colleague Crispinus mortally wounded. Hannibal honours the body; accounts differ on whether the ashes reached his son, and Plutarch closes with his dedications and descendants.","ideas":["Bold engagement versus Fabian caution","Shame of defeat turned into renewed attack","Rashness and ill omens lead to his death","Hannibal's honour to his fallen enemy"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"An emergency magistrate named by a consul; Plutarch derives the title from dicere, to name or declare."}]},{"i":477,"j":483,"loc":"Comparison of Pelopidas and Marcellus, chs. 1–3","title":"Pelopidas and Marcellus weighed against each other","summary":"Plutarch finds the two men alike in valour and magnanimity, differing chiefly in that Marcellus slaughtered the people of cities he took. He sets Leuctra, Tegyra and the liberation of Thebes against Marcellus's victory over the Gauls and his capture of Syracuse, and credits Marcellus with restoring Roman courage against Hannibal. He then censures both for throwing their lives away, finding Pelopidas more excusable, since he was driven by anger and the hope of killing the tyrant, while Marcellus died like a scout. The comparison ends by contrasting Pelopidas's burial by grateful allies with Marcellus's burial by admiring enemies.","ideas":["Marcellus was harsh to conquered cities; Pelopidas and Epaminondas were not","Marcellus taught Romans to be ashamed of surviving defeat","A general should not squander his life, since his death harms his country","Honour from enemies rests on worth alone"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The formal side-by-side judgement of a Greek and a Roman life that closes each pair of Lives."}]},{"i":484,"j":500,"loc":"Life of Aristides, chs. 1–4","title":"Aristides' wealth, character and rivalry with Themistocles","summary":"Plutarch weighs the evidence on whether Aristides was poor, rejecting Demetrius of Phalerum's arguments from the archonship, the ostracism and a choregic tripod. Aristides, a friend of Cleisthenes and admirer of Lycurgus, favours aristocracy and is from boyhood opposed to Themistocles, possibly through rivalry over the youth Stesilaüs. He refuses to build a faction of friends, sometimes withdraws his own measures or has others propose them, and is seen in the theatre as the man of Aeschylus's verse who wishes to be just, not merely seem so. As overseer of revenues he exposes embezzlement, is convicted through Themistocles, and then rebukes the Athenians for praising him once he let thieves alone.","ideas":["The evidence for Aristides' wealth is weak","Aristides refused to rely on a political faction","Justice even toward enemies in court","Honour for tolerating theft is more shameful than condemnation"],"terms":[{"t":"Pentacosiomedimni (Five-hundred-bushellers)","d":"The highest Athenian property class, from which archons were said to be drawn by lot."},{"t":"choregus","d":"A citizen who paid for and produced a chorus at a public festival."}]},{"i":501,"j":517,"loc":"Life of Aristides, chs. 5–10","title":"Marathon, ostracism, Salamis and the answer to Persia","summary":"At Marathon Aristides yields his day of command to Miltiades, fights beside Themistocles, and guards the spoils honestly while Callias the Torch-bearer secretly takes buried gold. Plutarch reflects on his surname 'The Just' and argues that virtue is the only divine excellence within human reach. Themistocles' charges lead to his ostracism, and Plutarch describes the procedure and the illiterate voter who asked Aristides to write his own name. Recalled before Xerxes' invasion, Aristides urges Themistocles to fight at Salamis, clears Psyttaleia, opposes cutting the Hellespont bridges, and moves the Athenian refusal of Mardonius's offers and the rebuke of Sparta.","ideas":["Obeying a wiser colleague is honourable, not shameful","Divinity has incorruption, power and virtue; only virtue is open to men","Ostracism vents envy without irreparable harm","Old rivals reconcile to save Hellas at Salamis"],"terms":[{"t":"ostrakon","d":"The potsherd on which each voter wrote the name of the citizen he wished banished for ten years."},{"t":"Dikaios ('The Just')","d":"The surname Aristides earned, which Plutarch calls kingly and godlike."}]},{"i":518,"j":537,"loc":"Life of Aristides, chs. 11–18","title":"The campaign of Plataea up to the battle","summary":"Aristides leads eight thousand Athenians to Plataea, where a Delphic oracle seems to require battle on Attic soil until a dream of Arimnestus reveals an old shrine of Eleusinian Demeter near Hysiae and the Plataeans cede their border land to Athens. Aristides settles the Tegeans' quarrel over the left wing, quietly handles an oligarchic conspiracy in the camp, and sends picked men to rescue the Megarians, who kill the Persian cavalry leader Masistius. Alexander of Macedon warns him at night of Mardonius's attack, and the Athenians agree to change wings with the Spartans. During a confused withdrawal the Spartans, held back by Amompharetus and bad omens, endure Persian arrows until Pausanias's prayer brings favourable sacrifices and they charge.","ideas":["Oracle and dream fixed the battlefield near Plataea","Valour does not depend on one's place in the line","Lenient handling of the conspirators turned the war into their trial","Spartan restraint under fire while awaiting omens"],"terms":[{"t":"medising","d":"Taking the side of the Medes, that is, the Persians, as the Thebans did."}]},{"i":538,"j":551,"loc":"Life of Aristides, chs. 18–21","title":"Victory at Plataea and its commemorations","summary":"The Athenians, marching to help Pausanias, are attacked by the medising Greeks and defeat the Thebans, while the Spartans rout the Persians and Arimnestus kills Mardonius with a stone, as an oracle had foretold. The Athenians then help storm the Persian stockade, and Plutarch disputes Herodotus's claim that only three cities fought. Aristides averts a quarrel over the prize of valour, which goes to Plataea; Euchidas runs to Delphi and back in a day for pure fire and dies on arrival. Aristides proposes the yearly council at Plataea, the Eleutheria games and a standing Greek force, and Plutarch describes the Plataeans' annual funeral rite for the fallen.","ideas":["Plataea was a common Greek victory, against Herodotus","The prize of valour went to Plataea to prevent civil strife","Fire polluted by the barbarians was rekindled from Delphi","Yearly rites honour men who died for freedom"],"terms":[{"t":"Eleutheria","d":"The festival games of deliverance that Aristides proposed holding at Plataea every fourth year."},{"t":"Zeus Eleutherios (the Deliverer)","d":"The god to whom the Greeks built an altar for the victory."}]},{"i":552,"j":565,"loc":"Life of Aristides, chs. 22–27","title":"Aristides' later policy, poverty, death and descendants","summary":"Back in Athens Aristides opens the archonship to all citizens and judges Themistocles' secret plan to burn the allied fleet most advantageous and most unjust, so the people reject it. With Cimon he wins the allies away from the harsh Pausanias and fixes the tribute of the league at 460 talents, coming home poorer than before. Plutarch notes, following Theophrastus, that Aristides was strictly just in private but bent to Athenian advantage in public matters, and tells how he testified for Callias that he chose his poverty. Plutarch rejects Craterus's story of a bribery conviction and closes with his public funeral, the city's grants to his poor children and grandchildren, and the tradition that Socrates married his granddaughter Myrto.","ideas":["Most advantageous yet most unjust: the fleet plan rejected","Leadership of the allies passed to Athens by tact","The just assessment of the tribute","Poverty borne by choice is a ground of pride"],"terms":[{"t":"phoros (tribute)","d":"The yearly contribution of the allied cities, first assessed by Aristides."}]},{"i":566,"j":576,"loc":"Life of Cato the Elder, chs. 1–3","title":"Cato's origins, frugality and entry into politics","summary":"Cato, of Tusculan stock and a 'new man', is described in appearance and in his early life as soldier and unpaid advocate in the country towns. He models himself on Manius Curius, who refused Samnite gold while cooking turnips, and is drawn further to austerity by the Pythagorean Nearchus at Tarentum, though he learns Greek only late. His neighbour Valerius Flaccus, hearing of his labour and frugality, brings him to Rome, where he rises through tribune and quaestor to consul and censor. As quaestor he quarrels with Scipio over extravagance and joins Fabius in denouncing him, but Scipio clears himself and sails for Africa.","ideas":["A 'new man' in office but old in ancestral valour","The example of Manius Curius and his turnips","Valerius Flaccus as patron of Cato's career","Early hostility to Scipio's lavishness"],"terms":[{"t":"novus homo (new man)","d":"A Roman who rose to office by his own merit without distinguished ancestors."},{"t":"catus","d":"Latin for wise and prudent, the source of the surname Cato."}]},{"i":577,"j":589,"loc":"Life of Cato the Elder, chs. 4–9","title":"Cato's manner of life and famous sayings","summary":"Cato's oratory earns him the name of a Roman Demosthenes, but his plain dress, cheap food and hard work are what make him rare as Rome grows large and luxurious. Plutarch objects to his selling of aged slaves and lists cases of kindness to worn-out animals, arguing that kindness reaches further than justice. He praises Cato's thrift and strict justice as governor of Sardinia, compares his oratory to Socrates' rough exterior, and gathers many of his sayings. These include the belly that has no ears, the king as a carnivorous animal, and the embassy with neither feet, head nor heart.","ideas":["Austerity kept up into old age, like an athlete's regimen","Plutarch condemns selling slaves once they are old","Kindness to animals as practice for kindness to men","Character shows in speech: a collection of Cato's sayings"],"terms":[{"t":"philanthropia (humanity, kindness)","d":"Plutarch's measure here: a goodwill that extends beyond strict justice, even to dumb beasts."}]},{"i":590,"j":602,"loc":"Life of Cato the Elder, chs. 10–15","title":"Consul in Spain, Thermopylae, and the prosecutor","summary":"As consul in Hither Spain Cato hires Celtiberian allies, wins a complete victory, razes the walls of many cities, and keeps no booty for himself. Superseded by Scipio, he still subdues the Lacetanians on his way home, and the Senate upholds his arrangements. As tribune under Manius Acilius against Antiochus he works on Greek cities, speaks to the Athenians through an interpreter, and at Thermopylae leads a night march over the heights that routs the king's army, which he boasts of loudly. In politics he prosecutes Scipio and his brother Lucius, and himself faces nearly fifty lawsuits up to the age of ninety.","ideas":["Better that many soldiers bring home silver than a few gold","A flanking march over Callidromus wins Thermopylae","Greek words are born on the lips, Roman in the heart","Cato's life of prosecutions given and endured"],"terms":[]},{"i":603,"j":612,"loc":"Life of Cato the Elder, chs. 16–19","title":"Cato's censorship and war on luxury","summary":"Plutarch explains the censor's powers over the lives of citizens, and how the nobles put up seven mild candidates against Cato, who promised a great purification with Valerius Flaccus. Elected, he expels Lucius Quintius Flamininus from the Senate for having a man beheaded at a banquet to please his favourite, and another senator for embracing his wife before their daughter. He taxes luxuries at ten times their value, cuts off private water pipes and demolishes encroachments, provoking Titus Flamininus to have his contracts annulled. The people nonetheless honour him with a statue for restoring the tottering state, though Cato himself scorned statues and praised himself freely.","ideas":["The censor watches private life as revealing character","Voters chose the severe physician over the agreeable","Expulsion of Lucius Flamininus for the banquet killing","Heavy assessments to check extravagance"],"terms":[{"t":"censor","d":"Roman magistrate who reviewed the Senate and knights, assessed property and supervised morals."}]},{"i":613,"j":628,"loc":"Life of Cato the Elder, chs. 20–27","title":"Cato at home, against Greek learning, and Carthage","summary":"Cato educates his son himself, as reader, law tutor and athletic trainer, and the son later distinguishes himself under Paulus Aemilius. He manages slaves by keeping them busy, divided and under strict discipline, and turns from farming to safe investments and maritime lending. He has the philosophers Carneades and Diogenes sent home quickly, mocks Socrates and Isocrates, distrusts Greek physicians, and predicts that Greek letters will ruin Rome, which Plutarch says time disproved. Widowed, he marries the young daughter of his clerk Salonius; finally, after an embassy to Carthage, he ends every speech demanding Carthage's destruction and dies as the third war begins, praising the young Scipio.","ideas":["A father's personal education of his son","Hostility to Greek philosophy and medicine","The scandalous late marriage to Salonius's daughter","Carthage must be destroyed; Nasica argues to spare it"],"terms":[{"t":"Carthago delenda","d":"Cato's refrain that, in his opinion, Carthage must be destroyed."}]},{"i":629,"j":643,"loc":"Comparison of Aristides and Cato, chs. 1–6","title":"Aristides and Cato compared","summary":"Plutarch notes that both rose by their own merit, but Cato faced harder rivals in a vaster Rome and won his offices and victories more in his own right, while Aristides shared his battles with Miltiades, Themistocles and Pausanias. He grants that Cato excelled in household management, the basis of a city, while Aristides' poverty left his descendants needy. He then argues that poverty chosen for public service is noble and that contentment, not wealth, is the true equipment of a statesman, asking why Cato boasted of frugality while amassing money. Aristides' wars were greater, and he was free from Cato's ambition, self-praise, persecution of Scipio and unseemly second marriage.","ideas":["Cato's rise was harder; Aristides' battles were greater","Household management is part of political skill","Freedom from wants is the most god-like human quality","Ambition and self-praise mar a statesman's gentleness"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkeia (contented independence)","d":"Needing no superfluities, which Plutarch calls the best equipment for public service."}]},{"i":644,"j":654,"loc":"Life of Philopoemen, chs. 1–7","title":"Philopoemen's education and first exploits","summary":"Raised by the exile Cleander and trained by Ecdemus and Megalophanes, pupils of Arcesilaüs, Philopoemen is later called the last of the Greeks. Plain and indifferent to appearances, he imitates Epaminondas's energy but not his mildness, rejects athletics as harmful to soldiering, works his farm, and studies tactics on the ground itself. When Cleomenes seizes Megalopolis he covers the citizens' escape and dissuades them from accepting Cleomenes' offer. At Sellasia he charges without orders, pulls a javelin from both thighs and fights on, then trains in Crete and reforms the Achaean cavalry, killing the Eleian commander Damophantus at the Larissus.","ideas":["The last of the Greeks","An athlete's regimen is useless for war","The unordered charge at Sellasia wins Antigonus's praise","Drill turns lax knights into a responsive cavalry"],"terms":[{"t":"Achaean league","d":"The federation of Peloponnesian cities whose cavalry and later whole army Philopoemen led."}]},{"i":655,"j":668,"loc":"Life of Philopoemen, chs. 8–13","title":"Reforming the Achaeans and defeating Machanidas","summary":"Plutarch traces the Achaean league from Aratus, who relied on kings, to Philopoemen, under whom it fought alone. Philopoemen rearms the Achaeans with long pike and heavy shield and turns their love of luxury toward splendid armour. At Mantineia he lets the tyrant Machanidas pursue the routed light troops, crushes the Spartan phalanx and kills Machanidas at a ditch, and at the Nemean games the theatre acclaims him. His name alone makes the Boeotians lift a siege and Nabis abandon Messene, but his second stay in Crete, while Megalopolis suffers, brings charges of desertion and a feud with his city.","ideas":["Display turned from banquets to arms strengthens courage","Machanidas's pursuit opens the gap that ruins him","Philopoemen's reputation alone relieves Megara and Messene","Absence in Crete brings calumny at home"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"A close-ordered body of heavy infantry, which Philopoemen rearmed on the Macedonian model."}]},{"i":669,"j":676,"loc":"Life of Philopoemen, chs. 14–16","title":"War with Nabis and mastery over Sparta","summary":"Returning from Crete, Philopoemen is chosen general against Nabis, loses a sea battle through inexperience and a rotten old ship, then burns the enemy camp at Gythium and wins by a skilful change of order and night ambushes. His fame irritates Titus Flamininus. After Nabis's murder he brings Sparta into the league and refuses the Spartans' gift of 120 talents, advising them to bribe their bad men instead. Later, as a private man, he shuts Diophanes and Flamininus out of Sparta, but afterwards as general he executes Spartans, razes the walls and abolishes the Lycurgan training, which the Spartans restore later with Roman permission.","ideas":["Skill on land does not carry over to the sea","Refusal of the Spartan gift: buy enemies' silence, not friends'","A lawless but great-spirited act saves Sparta","Anger leads him to destroy Lycurgus's discipline"],"terms":[{"t":"agōgē (Lycurgan training)","d":"The traditional Spartan system of rearing boys, which Philopoemen abolished."}]},{"i":677,"j":686,"loc":"Life of Philopoemen, chs. 17–21","title":"Resisting Rome; capture and death at Messene","summary":"As Rome's power closes round Greece after Antiochus's defeat, Philopoemen yields at times but mostly opposes, rebuking the pro-Roman Aristaenus and insisting that the Spartan exiles owe their return to the Achaeans. At seventy, in his eighth generalship, he rides from a sickbed to stop Deinocrates' revolt of Messene, covers his young horsemen's retreat, falls from his horse, and is taken prisoner. Shut in an underground chamber, he is given poison at night, asks whether Lycortas and the horsemen escaped, and dies quietly. The Achaeans avenge him and carry his ashes home with Polybius, and later Mummius refuses a Roman's demand to remove his statues.","ideas":["Like a helmsman against a high sea, he resists Rome","Sacrifice of himself to save his young horsemen","His last concern is for his men","Honours to a benefactor upheld even by Romans"],"terms":[]},{"i":687,"j":695,"loc":"Life of Flamininus, chs. 1–5","title":"Flamininus's character, consulship and the Apsus pass","summary":"Titus Quintius Flamininus, quick to anger and to favour but more lasting in kindness, serves under Marcellus and governs Tarentum before seeking the consulship under thirty. Plutarch argues that Greece needed a commander who would win by persuasion and justice. Refusing to waste his year at Rome, he takes Scipio's veterans to Epirus, where herdsmen lead a detachment round Philip's position on the Apsus and a smoke signal triggers a combined assault that drives the Macedonians off. His troops refrain from plundering, cities come over, and the Greeks find in him a young, humane man who speaks Greek; Philip rejects his terms of freedom for the Greeks.","ideas":["Ambition for honour, but generosity to those he helped","Persuasion rather than force was needed in Greece","A flanking march by herdsmen's path forces the pass","Restraint in Epirus wins the Greek cities"],"terms":[]},{"i":696,"j":707,"loc":"Life of Flamininus, chs. 6–9","title":"Thebes, Cynoscephalae and peace with Philip","summary":"Flamininus enters Thebes by stealth with his soldiers behind him and wins over the Boeotians, while King Attalus collapses mid-speech. His command is extended, and near Scotussa the two armies meet; Philip's exhortation from a burial mound dismays his men. In mist on the hills of Cynoscephalae the Macedonian right wins, but Titus breaks the left on rough ground where the phalanx cannot hold together, and the whole army flees, though Aetolian looting lets Philip escape. Annoyed by Aetolian boasts and Alcaeus's epigram, Titus makes peace on his own terms, keeping Philip out of Greece so that he and Antiochus cannot unite.","ideas":["The phalanx is invincible only while it stays one body","The Aetolians claim the victory and vex Titus","A timely peace separated Philip's war from Antiochus's","Hannibal at Antiochus's court as the coming danger"],"terms":[{"t":"Cynoscephalae ('Dog's Heads')","d":"The line of sharp hilltops in Thessaly where Philip was defeated."}]},{"i":708,"j":717,"loc":"Life of Flamininus, chs. 10–12","title":"The proclamation of Greek freedom at the Isthmus","summary":"Stung by Aetolian taunts about the 'fetters of Greece', Titus persuades the commissioners to free Corinth, Chalcis and Demetrias as well. At the Isthmian games a herald proclaims the Greek peoples free, ungarrisoned and untaxed, and the shout is so loud that ravens fall from the sky, which Plutarch explains physically. That night the Greeks reflect that foreigners had freed them almost without bloodshed, whereas Greek generals had won battles only to enslave Greece. Titus follows his words with deeds, sending officers to free cities, presiding at the Nemean games, reconciling factions, and dedicating offerings at Delphi as liberator.","ideas":["Freedom for the 'fetters' as well as the rest of Greece","Ravens fall at the shout of the crowd","The rarest blessing is a just man","Gratitude brought Rome trust and power"],"terms":[{"t":"Isthmian games","d":"The festival at Corinth where the freedom of the Greeks was proclaimed, as Nero did again later."}]},{"i":718,"j":725,"loc":"Life of Flamininus, chs. 13–17","title":"Nabis, the freed captives, Antiochus and Chalcis","summary":"Titus makes peace with Nabis instead of capturing him, perhaps from fear of a successor or jealousy of Philopoemen, though he claims to have spared Sparta. The Achaeans ransom twelve hundred Roman captives sold in Greece and give them to him, and they follow his triumph with shaved heads among rich spoils. When Antiochus invades with Aetolian help, Titus serves under Manius Acilius, wins over wavering cities, and after Thermopylae persuades the consul to grant the Aetolians a truce and to spare Chalcis. The Chalcidians still sing hymns to him; Plutarch closes with examples of his quick wit.","ideas":["Peace with Nabis disappoints Greek hopes","Freed Roman captives as the best gift","Intercession for Naupactus and Chalcis","Antiochus's varied troops are all Syrians"],"terms":[]},{"i":726,"j":735,"loc":"Life of Flamininus, chs. 18–21","title":"Censorship, his brother's disgrace, and Hannibal's death","summary":"As censor Titus names Scipio first senator but becomes Cato's enemy when Cato expels his brother Lucius for having a condemned man beheaded at a banquet; Plutarch compares the versions of the story and blames Titus's lasting hatred. In old age his ambition leads him, as envoy to Prusias, to demand Hannibal, who, having taken an oracle about Libyssan earth to mean Libya, finds the king's guards at his escape tunnels and takes his own life. Many Romans condemn Titus and contrast Scipio's generosity, including the conversation at Ephesus about the greatest generals. Others defend him by the later risings of Aristonicus and Mithridates and the return of Marius, and Titus ends his life in peace.","ideas":["Family loyalty sets Titus against a just censor","The oracle of Libyssa fulfilled in Bithynia","Scipio's magnanimity contrasted with Titus's harshness","Fortune can revive a fallen enemy"],"terms":[]},{"i":736,"j":742,"loc":"Comparison of Philopoemen and Titus, chs. 1–3","title":"Philopoemen and Titus compared","summary":"Plutarch grants that Titus's benefactions to Greece were the greater, since he freed Greek cities as a foreigner while Philopoemen fought Greeks. Titus erred from ambition, Philopoemen from contentious anger, which made him undo his kindness to Sparta and rush to his death at Messene. Philopoemen, however, had more battles, made his soldiers good, invented his own reforms, and showed personal prowess, and he led even as a private citizen. Plutarch leaves the verdict to the reader, proposing the crown for generalship to the Greek and for justice and goodness to the Roman.","ideas":["Titus freed Greece; Philopoemen fought Greeks","Ambition versus contentiousness as their faults","One commanded good soldiers, the other made them","Generalship to the Greek, justice to the Roman"],"terms":[]}]});